cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this research software, cite the all-versions Zenodo record; use the v1.1.0 version DOI once issued."
title: "The Discrete Jacobian Program: A Lean-Checked Splice Collision and a Rigidity Theorem for Graph Rewriting"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Edwin
    family-names: Rosero
version: 1.1.0
date-released: 2026-08-12
abstract: >-
  Research software and explicit certificates showing that own-comatch
  undoability, together with a bounded semantic-D1 screen, can coexist with
  a global collision on graph-isomorphism classes. The release includes executable search
  code, independently replayed certificates, bounded exhaustive sweeps, hand
  proofs, and a Lean 4/Mathlib formalization of the concrete flagship
  collision. Within the declared census, 489 rules were enumerated, 238
  passed the bounded semantic-D1 screen, and 52 collided within
  four vertices and three edges, increasing to 182 within four edges. These
  figures are bounded evidence. Version 1.1.0 adds an elementary hand proof
  that exact semantic D1 together with exact history unambiguity
  implies injectivity of one-step evolution on isomorphism classes. The
  general rigidity proof is not yet formalized in Lean or externally peer
  reviewed, and its converse remains open. “Discrete Jacobian” is an
  analogy; no result about the classical polynomial Jacobian conjecture is
  claimed.
keywords:
  - graph rewriting
  - directed multigraphs
  - discrete dynamics
  - application-level undoability
  - global injectivity
  - discrete Jacobian
  - history ambiguity
  - Lean 4
  - Wolfram model
  - exhaustive search
  - multiway systems
